<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 17" label="MODULE 17 / SMALL NETWORK DESIGN AND TROUBLESHOOTING" /><h1>Build the network.<br /><span>Prove it works.</span></h1><p>A small network combines endpoint devices, switches, routers, wireless access, addressing, and application services. A reliable build includes a clear plan and a repeatable verification process.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Plan the small network</h2><p>Identify the required users, applications, devices, addressing, wireless coverage, Internet access, and security needs. Select suitable switches, routers, access points, and media. Use a logical topology to document VLANs, subnets, interfaces, and default gateways, and a physical topology to document device placement and cabling.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1419 · IMG-1399 · IMG-1472</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Scale without losing control</h2><p>As a network grows, use hierarchical design, structured addressing, documented configurations, and consistent security policy. Plan for additional users, services, and traffic. Standardized naming, configuration backups, and monitoring make a network easier to maintain and troubleshoot.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1426 · IMG-1413</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Use a six-step troubleshooting method</h2><p>A repeatable process prevents random configuration changes and makes the result easier to verify:</p><ol><li><strong>Identify the problem.</strong> Gather symptoms from the user, define the scope, and establish what is and is not working.</li><li><strong>Establish a theory of probable causes.</strong> Use the evidence to list likely explanations, starting with the simplest relevant fault domain.</li><li><strong>Test the theory.</strong> Run a targeted test. If it does not confirm the theory, gather more information and revise it.</li><li><strong>Establish a plan and implement the solution.</strong> Choose a corrective action based on the confirmed cause, then apply it deliberately.</li><li><strong>Verify the solution and apply preventive measures.</strong> Test full functionality, including the original failing service, and reduce the chance of recurrence where practical.</li><li><strong>Document findings and outcomes.</strong> Record symptoms, evidence, cause, actions, and verification results.</li></ol><p>Work from the local connection outward: physical link and interface state, host addressing, default gateway, routed path, DNS, and finally the application. Do not assume a failed ping alone identifies the faulty device.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1428 · IMG-1407 · IMG-1423 · IMG-1466 · IMG-1468 · IMG-1481 · IMG-1482</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">04</div><div><h2>Compare performance with a network baseline</h2><p>A baseline records normal network behavior so current measurements have context. Compare current ping round-trip times, utilization, and traffic patterns with measurements from a similar time and operating condition. A large change can indicate added delay or an abnormal workload even when connectivity still succeeds.</p><p>When planning an upgrade, a protocol analyzer can capture traffic on the relevant network segments. Analyze the traffic to document the applications, sources, destinations, and bandwidth requirements instead of estimating capacity from a single speed test.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Network baseline and traffic analysis</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">05</div><div><h2>Choose IOS commands that answer the question</h2><p>Use concise verification commands before changing configuration. <code>show ip interface brief</code> summarizes IPv4 addresses and interface state; <code>show interfaces</code> exposes counters, errors, and link details; <code>show ip route</code> displays installed routes; and <code>show cdp neighbors</code> helps confirm directly connected Cisco devices and port mappings.</p><p>IOS extended ping and extended traceroute let an administrator choose a source address or interface. This matters when testing reachability from the same interface a remote host uses, especially when the return path may be asymmetric. Start the interactive extended mode by entering <code>ping</code> or <code>traceroute</code> without a destination in privileged EXEC mode.</p><p>Use <code>debug</code> commands sparingly. Target the specific event, monitor output, and disable debugging with <code>undebug all</code> when finished because excessive debug output can consume device resources.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE MATERIAL</span><code>Module 17 · Host and IOS commands</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-lab"><div class="lesson-number">LAB</div><div><h2>Follow a repeatable troubleshooting path</h2><p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A user can reach a public IP address but cannot open a website by name. Start with the endpoint and move outward.</p><ol><li>On Windows, run <code>ipconfig /all</code>. Check the IPv4 address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server. An address in <code>169.254.0.0/16</code> suggests the host did not obtain a DHCP lease.</li><li>Run <code>ping 127.0.0.1</code> to test the local TCP/IP stack, then ping the host's own address and default gateway.</li><li>Run <code>ping 1.1.1.1</code>. If this succeeds but names fail, basic IP connectivity is working and DNS becomes a strong suspect.</li><li>Run <code>nslookup example.com</code> to query the configured DNS server. Check the server address and whether the query times out or returns an incorrect result.</li><li>Use <code>tracert 1.1.1.1</code> to inspect the path if remote connectivity is failing. A timeout at one intermediate hop is not proof of a forwarding failure if later hops still respond.</li></ol><p class="lab-note">On Cisco IOS, pair host tests with <code>show ip interface brief</code>, <code>show interfaces</code>, and <code>show ip route</code>. Verify the fix with the original failing application, not just a successful ping.</p></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Build a Small Network</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
